# HTTP security headers for cadetai.co.uk
#
# ============================================================================
#  READ THIS FIRST: NONE OF THIS FILE IS LIVE TODAY.
# ============================================================================
# The site is hosted on GitHub Pages. GitHub Pages does not read `_headers`
# and offers no way to set custom response headers, so every header below is
# currently INERT. This file is kept as the single source of truth for the day
# the site sits behind a header-capable host or proxy.
#
# What that means in practice, split honestly:
#
#   ENFORCED TODAY (in-page <meta>, works on GitHub Pages)
#     - Content-Security-Policy  ... via <meta http-equiv> in every .html
#     - Referrer-Policy          ... via <meta name="referrer"> in every .html
#
#   NOT ENFORCED TODAY (header-only, needs a proxy - no <meta> equivalent
#   exists, and browsers ignore these if you try to fake them in a meta tag)
#     - Strict-Transport-Security   (HSTS)
#     - X-Frame-Options             (clickjacking)
#     - X-Content-Type-Options      (MIME sniffing)
#     - Permissions-Policy          (feature policy)
#     - CSP `frame-ancestors`       (clickjacking; explicitly ignored in meta)
#
# So today the site has NO clickjacking protection, NO HSTS and NO nosniff.
# `frame-ancestors` and HSTS in particular CANNOT be fixed from the repo -
# they require a proxy. Deliberately not adding meta tags for them: browsers
# ignore them in meta form, so they would only create false assurance.
#
# To make this file live (either option is free):
#   1. Put Cloudflare in front of GitHub Pages: enable HSTS in the dashboard
#      and add a Response-Header Transform Rule with the values below; or
#   2. Move hosting to Cloudflare Pages / Netlify - this file then applies
#      as-is, no extra config.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Note on the CSP below: it is deliberately the UNION of what every page needs
# (i.e. it includes the TikTok allowances that only the homepage uses). Each
# page also carries its own, tighter meta CSP. When a header CSP and a meta
# CSP are both present the browser enforces BOTH (the intersection), so the
# per-page meta policy still wins - a page never gets loosened by this header.
# Making the header narrower than the homepage needs would break the embed.
#
# Per-page meta CSP variants actually shipped (see each file's <head>):
#   index.html ........... baseline. It used to carry the TikTok creator embed
#                          (script/style from *.ttwstatic.com, frame from
#                          www.tiktok.com); M-11 removed the embed and its meta
#                          CSP is now frame-src 'none' with no third-party
#                          origins, same as the rest.
#   app.html ............. strictest: script-src 'none', style-src 'self'
#   404.html ............. script-src 'none' (page loads no JS)
#   privacy / terms / delete-account ... script-src 'self' (no inline script)
#   all other pages ...... script-src 'self' + sha256 of the one inline
#                          `documentElement.className += ' js'` flag script
#
# 'unsafe-inline' appears in style-src only, and only because ~200 inline
# style="..." attributes exist across the markup. Modern browsers confine it
# to style-src-attr; style-src-elem stays hash/host-locked so an injected
# <style> block is still blocked. There is no 'unsafe-inline' or 'unsafe-eval'
# anywhere in script-src, on any page.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIRD-PARTY ORIGINS REMOVED 2026-08-14 (M-11 residual).
#
# M-11 removed the TikTok script and Google Fonts from the pages, but this CSP
# still PERMITTED them: frame-src and script-src named www.tiktok.com and
# *.ttwstatic.com, style-src named fonts.googleapis.com, font-src named
# fonts.gstatic.com. Inert only because GitHub Pages ignores this file (L-07) --
# which meant the moment L-07 was remediated with a CDN, the policy would have
# re-permitted exactly the hosts M-11 was raised to remove, on a site aimed at
# children. A control that is only safe because it is not enforced is not a
# control.
#
# Verified before removing: the only remaining references to tiktok.com are
# outbound <a href> links and JSON-LD sameAs entries, neither of which loads
# anything or needs a CSP allowance. There is no @font-face and no fonts
# directory -- the site uses system fonts (system-ui, -apple-system, "Segoe UI",
# Roboto) -- so font-src is 'none'.
#
# scripts/no-third-party-runtime.test.mjs walked only .html/.css/.js and never
# read this file, so nothing would have caught the drift. It now scans it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/*
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains; preload
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; object-src 'none'; frame-src 'none'; worker-src 'none'; manifest-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'sha256-DamCoPgB/VkDCPsu0R6X4sJsz1c66vGlUGBXLu04Yxg='; script-src-attr 'none'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src-elem 'self'; style-src-attr 'unsafe-inline'; font-src 'none'; img-src 'self' data:; media-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests
